r resolution: year, month, day, hours, minutes, seconds. ","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"PfoftFb6Md"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"ISO 8601","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"IynacSEL62"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601","data":{"page":"ISO_8601","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"Tx0aVv0r0p"},{"type":"text","value":" provides a textual representation or microformat which has been adopted or implemented in most encoding and software systems.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"ZZMOrJjZ42"}],"key":"PqSoXgwQrM"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: \"2023-10-04T12:13:30.25+02:00\"","key":"eGzLoUhLE7"}],"key":"exI8ImJD0s"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This should be understood as a multi-valued tuple providing values for each component of the calendar and clock. Conversion to a coordinate on a time-line can be done arithmetically except that a look-up is required to convert months to their durations in days or seconds, etc. The final two elements of the value, following ‘+’ or ‘-’, indicate the timezone, encoded as the hours and minutes offset from ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"EcZHzQIJwv"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC_offset","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"UTC","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"l0smb2Re08"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC_offset","data":{"page":"UTC_offset","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"I2UIL2UHvU"},{"type":"text","value":". ISO 8601 uses the Gregorian calendar, whose epoch is the beginning of Year 1 CE.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"G3BCgmPmtW"}],"key":"dTctp0uoYq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"While other calendars are in contemporary use within some specific communities and for some cultural applications (e.g. Traditional Chinese, Julian, Islamic, Jewish, Baha’i), the Gregorian calendar and the 24-hour clock are universally used for technical purposes.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"Bcpowmsua1"}],"key":"Y45XuXWdQ8"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered nominal timescales","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"NWNhA1BomW"}],"identifier":"ordered-nominal-timescales","label":"Ordered nominal timescales","html_id":"ordered-nominal-timescales","implicit":true,"key":"eqYApXK27I"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In historical, archeological, and geological applications, dates may be expressed using a named period, tied to a recognised culture (e.g., ‘bronze age’), dynasty (e.g., ‘Tudor’), or defined by some event(s) observed in the geological record tied to the natural history of a region or the earth (e.g., ‘Proterozoic’). Some of these systems can be mapped to temporal coordinates, though calibrations may be adjusted according to new evidence156. In general, however, only the ordering relationships between members of a nominal timescale are defined - i.e. we know that a nominal date is before, after, or during some other date within the same\nsystem, but not the size of the separation between them.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"CfwTp80u6N"}],"key":"mAR7sv0uRF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are many nominal systems. The ","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"jKm7FRz0mt"},{"type":"link","url":"https://stratigraphy.org/chart","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"International Chronostratigraphic Chart","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"zlmpG2afw3"}],"urlSource":"https://stratigraphy.org/chart","key":"RnRprjHx17"},{"type":"text","value":" and its calibration is authoritatively maintained by the ","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"ura1uHfiqi"},{"type":"link","url":"https://stratigraphy.org/","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"International Commission on Stratigraphy","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"LiWvpvFWLr"}],"urlSource":"https://stratigraphy.org/","key":"TsyGTjl8ok"},{"type":"text","value":", and provides the temporal reference system used internationally in geology. Most other nominal systems are only used locally, within particular disciplinary communities.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"ceVFIIMK4p"}],"key":"qKa0ksVvHz"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recurring and periodic times","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"OSkPQaujyJ"}],"identifier":"recurring-and-periodic-times","label":"Recurring and periodic times","html_id":"recurring-and-periodic-times","implicit":true,"key":"pvrhZJN6xh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many datasets are structured as time-series, with either regular or irregular sampling period, or frequency of observation. A sequence of time-stamps may be thought of as a one-dimensional grid.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"benH3JW4o9"}],"key":"CKV3SAqk7R"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"YRiUNcs0NY"}],"identifier":"usage","label":"Usage","html_id":"usage","implicit":true,"key":"BprHg3noIn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Time series - the periodic measurement of the same phenomenon, using the same methods, etc. - are a common way of determining trends or otherwise making observations across time. In some cases, time series are the product of data integration from disparate sources, while in other cases the data are collected as part of time series as part of their design. In the Statistical Data and Metadata Exchange (SDMX) ","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"Y3cf434i15"},{"type":"link","url":"https://sdmx.org/?page_id=3215","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Content-Oriented Guidelines","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"pdeRenTjGi"}],"key":"gUyUzgu5gD"}],"urlSource":"https://sdmx.org/?page_id=3215","key":"xPfSSmGswm"},{"type":"text","value":" there is a standard enumeration of commonly used frequencies for national and supra-national official statistics. ","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"cXGJUAgyfX"},{"type":"link","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/70907.html#:~:text=This%20document%20specifies%20representations%20of,Coordinated%20Universal%20Time%20(UTC)","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"ISO 8601-1:2019","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"k1VFFufNme"}],"urlSource":"https://www.iso.org/standard/70907.html#:~:text=This%20document%20specifies%20representations%20of,Coordinated%20Universal%20Time%20(UTC)","key":"n3oa2tmrnB"},{"type":"text","value":" adds notation for recurring arbitrary intervals (e.g., 9-5, every Mon-Fri, etc.).","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"Npp7llALAq"}],"key":"pj7YK6XVmZ"}],"key":"jBvLjgNc2v"}],"key":"XORUT0ZqLB"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Geography","url":"/universals/univgeography","group":"Profiles/Recommendations"},"next":{"title":"Units of measurement","url":"/universals/univuom","group":"Profiles/Recommendations"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}